WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
How can one human choice make one river population rise while another falls?

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.960
Watch one Virginia river as people change who lives there and what each population

3
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:11.960
can use.

4
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.790
Start with one river edge.

5
00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.750
Green marsh plants provide habitat along the bank.

6
00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.970
Teal crabs and small fish move below.

7
00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.110
Gold white catfish are native here.

8
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.410
All the white catfish in this river form one population.

9
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.390
The shoreline shelters animals while the moving water carries food.

10
00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:40.163
People belong inside this ecosystem, too.

11
00:00:41.503 --> 00:00:45.263
They depend on it for food, water, energy and other resources.

12
00:00:46.683 --> 00:00:51.683
Their choices can change habitat, available resources, or which species are present.

13
00:00:53.023 --> 00:00:57.503
A habitat is where an organism finds the food, water and shelter it needs.

14
00:00:58.583 --> 00:01:01.683
Now people release blue catfish, shown in blue.

15
00:01:03.003 --> 00:01:06.263
These fish came from waters outside Virginia's Bay rivers.

16
00:01:07.663 --> 00:01:11.243
Here they spread and cause harm, so they are invasive, not merely new.

17
00:01:12.563 --> 00:01:13.803
Being new is not enough.

18
00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:19.707
Blue catfish eat plants and animals, including fish and sometimes blue crabs.

19
00:01:21.507 --> 00:01:23.887
Scientists report that white catfish eat similar prey.

20
00:01:25.207 --> 00:01:30.107
When species need the same limited resource, they may compete, even without a fight.

21
00:01:31.407 --> 00:01:34.347
One fish can simply eat food another fish needed.

22
00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:37.747
Both catfish now use the teal prey pool.

23
00:01:38.987 --> 00:01:43.687
If the blue population grows, what do you predict happens to the food remaining for

24
00:01:43.687 --> 00:01:44.687
white catfish?

25
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:53.953
More blue catfish feed, and the shared prey pool may shrink.

26
00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:57.213
Less food may remain for white catfish.

27
00:01:58.413 --> 00:02:03.573
That is a possible competition pathway, not proof of what caused a real population

28
00:02:03.573 --> 00:02:08.293
change. Other habitat changes may matter at the same time.

29
00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:12.247
People also fill part of the green marsh for homes.

30
00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:16.867
Habitat disappears, so some animals must move or die.

31
00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:22.767
Runoff enters the water, and extra fertilizer can later lower oxygen.

32
00:02:24.227 --> 00:02:26.887
Both changes may affect several local populations.

33
00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.310
Watch the whole river, not one arrow.

34
00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:32.670
Blue catfish rise.

35
00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.210
Shared prey and shoreline habitat change.

36
00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:42.670
Crabs, small fish and white catfish may respond differently because food-web effects

37
00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:46.790
spread. A change can reach species people catch and eat.

38
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:49.733
Human actions can help, too.

39
00:02:51.013 --> 00:02:54.353
People can restore habitat or remove some invasive fish.

40
00:02:55.713 --> 00:02:59.073
How far each population recovers is a question to test.

41
00:03:00.273 --> 00:03:02.613
A model lets us change one thing at a time.

42
00:03:03.617 --> 00:03:05.657
Here is the rule to carry forward.

43
00:03:06.957 --> 00:03:10.817
When people change an ecosystem, populations may rise or fall.

44
00:03:12.117 --> 00:03:16.657
The rule guides predictions, but evidence must show what changed in a real river.
